/* ── 1. What is NER ──────────────────────────────────────── */
+ '<h3 class="l-section-title">1 — What is Named Entity Recognition?</h3>'
+ '<p class="l-text">NER is a <strong>structured information extraction</strong> task. Instead of assigning one label to an entire sentence, we assign a label to every token. The labels identify <em>spans</em> of text that refer to real-world entities: people, places, organizations, dates, monetary values, and more. NER is the first step in most information extraction and knowledge graph construction pipelines.</p>'

+ '<div class="calc-cards">'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="cc-title" style="color:#4ecdc4">PER — Person</div><div class="cc-body">Names of individual humans. Examples: <em>Barack Obama</em>, <em>Marie Curie</em>, <em>Elon Musk</em>. In clinical NLP this also covers patient and physician names which must be de-identified.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="cc-title" style="color:#c8a96e">ORG — Organization</div><div class="cc-body">Companies, institutions, agencies, political parties. Examples: <em>OpenAI</em>, <em>MIT</em>, <em>WHO</em>, <em>NASA</em>. For financial NLP, extracting company names from earnings calls is a core task.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="cc-title" style="color:#4ecdc4">LOC — Location</div><div class="cc-body">Geographical locations, cities, countries, landmarks. Examples: <em>Paris</em>, <em>Mount Everest</em>, <em>the Amazon</em>. GPE (Geo-Political Entity) is a finer-grained version used in some annotation schemes.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="cc-title" style="color:#c8a96e">DATE / TIME</div><div class="cc-body">Temporal expressions. Examples: <em>January 2023</em>, <em>last Tuesday</em>, <em>three years ago</em>. Crucial for event extraction and timeline construction in news analysis.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="cc-title" style="color:#ff4b4b">MISC — Miscellaneous</div><div class="cc-body">Entities that do not fit standard categories: nationalities, events, works of art, products. Examples: <em>the Olympics</em>, <em>iPhone 15</em>, <em>French</em> (as adjective referring to France).</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="cc-title" style="color:#4ecdc4">Domain-Specific</div><div class="cc-body">Custom entity types for specialized domains: GENE, PROTEIN, DRUG, CHEMICAL in biomedical NLP; CLAUSE, PARTY, JURISDICTION in legal NLP; TICKER, INDEX, CURRENCY in financial NLP.</div></div>'
+ '</div>'

+ '<p class="l-text">Standard benchmarks: <strong>CoNLL-2003</strong> (English/German news, PER/ORG/LOC/MISC), <strong>OntoNotes 5</strong> (18 entity types, multiple genres), <strong>ACE 2005</strong> (7 types with nested entities). State-of-the-art BERT-based models score ~92–93 F1 on CoNLL-2003.</p>'

/* ── 2. BIO Tagging ──────────────────────────────────────── */
+ '<h3 class="l-section-title">2 — The BIO Tagging Scheme</h3>'
+ '<p class="l-text">Named entities often span multiple words ("New York City", "Steve Jobs"). To represent spans with per-token labels we use the <strong>BIO scheme</strong>: <strong>B</strong> = Beginning of an entity, <strong>I</strong> = Inside (continuation), <strong>O</strong> = Outside (not an entity). This encodes both the entity type and its boundaries in a flat label sequence.</p>'

+ '<div class="calc-example">'
+ '<div class="ce-title">BIO Annotation Example</div>'
+ '<p style="color:rgba(255,255,255,0.7);font-size:0.85rem;margin-bottom:12px">Sentence: "Steve Jobs founded Apple in Cupertino in 1976."</p>'
+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span> BIO Tag Assignment per Token <button class="code-copy">COPY</button></div><div class="code-block"><pre><code>'
+ 'Token       → Label\n'
+ '─────────────────────────────\n'
+ 'Steve       → B-PER   <span class="cm">(Beginning of Person)</span>\n'
+ 'Jobs        → I-PER   <span class="cm">(Inside Person)</span>\n'
+ 'founded     → O       <span class="cm">(Outside — not an entity)</span>\n'
+ 'Apple       → B-ORG   <span class="cm">(Beginning of Organization)</span>\n'
+ '<span class="kw">in</span>          → O\n'
+ 'Cupertino   → B-LOC   <span class="cm">(Beginning of Location)</span>\n'
+ '<span class="kw">in</span>          → O\n'
+ '<span class="num">1976</span>        → B-DATE  <span class="cm">(Beginning of Date)</span>\n'
+ '.           → O'
+ '</code></pre></div></div>'
+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>How to read this BIO trace:</strong> 1) "Steve" is the first token of a person, so it gets <code>B-PER</code>. 2) "Jobs" continues the same entity span, so it gets <code>I-PER</code>. 3) "founded" is not part of any entity, so it gets <code>O</code>. 4) "Apple" opens a new organization span (<code>B-ORG</code>), "Cupertino" opens a location (<code>B-LOC</code>) and "1976" opens a date (<code>B-DATE</code>); every non-entity word ("in", ".") gets <code>O</code>.</p>'
+ '<p style="color:rgba(255,255,255,0.6);font-size:0.82rem;margin-top:10px"><strong>Rule:</strong> Every new entity starts with B-, even if it directly follows an entity of the same type. This distinguishes "John Smith" (one PER) from "John Smith" followed by another "John" (two PERs).</p>'
+ '</div>'

+ '<p class="l-text">Variants of BIO: <strong>BIOES</strong> adds E- (End) and S- (Single-token entity) for richer boundary information. <strong>BILOU</strong> is equivalent (Begin, Inside, Last, Outside, Unit). Most transformer NER systems use plain BIO because BERT already captures context; the extra tags rarely help.</p>'

