/* ── 3. Subword Alignment ────────────────────────────────── */
+ '<h3 class="l-section-title">3 — The Subword Alignment Problem</h3>'
+ '<p class="l-text">Here is the core technical challenge of NER with transformers. Your dataset has word-level labels. Your tokenizer operates at the <em>subword</em> level. A word like "Washington" might become ["Wash", "##ington"] — two tokens sharing one label. A word like "WHO" might stay as ["WHO"] — one token, one label. You must <strong>align</strong> subword tokens to their original word labels before training.</p>'

+ '<div class="calc-highlight">'
+ '<strong>The Alignment Problem — Three Cases</strong><br><br>'
+ '<strong>Case 1: One word → multiple subword tokens</strong><br>'
+ 'Word: "Washington" (label: B-LOC)<br>'
+ 'Tokens: ["Wash", "##ington"]<br>'
+ 'Strategy: assign B-LOC to "Wash", assign <em>-100</em> (ignored) or I-LOC to "##ington"<br><br>'
+ '<strong>Case 2: Special tokens [CLS] and [SEP]</strong><br>'
+ 'These tokens have no corresponding word and must receive label <em>-100</em> so they are ignored in the loss.<br><br>'
+ '<strong>Case 3: One word → one token (common)</strong><br>'
+ 'Word: "Paris" (label: B-LOC) → Token: ["Paris"] → Label: B-LOC. No issue.<br><br>'
+ '<strong>Solution: use word_ids()</strong><br>'
+ 'tokenizer(...).word_ids() returns the word index (or None for special tokens) for every subword token. Iterate through word_ids to assign labels, setting -100 for None and for all but the first subword of each word.'
+ '</div>'

+ '<div class="calc-example">'
+ '<div class="ce-title">Subword Alignment — Visual Trace</div>'
+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span> Subword-to-Label Alignment Trace <button class="code-copy">COPY</button></div><div class="code-block"><pre><code>'
