+ 'Labels:     [B-PER,    I-PER,    O,         B-ORG ]\n'
+ '\n'
+ '<span class="cm"># After BERT tokenization:</span>\n'
+ 'Tokens:     [CLS, <span class="str">"Steve"</span>, <span class="str">"Jo"</span>, <span class="str">"##bs"</span>, <span class="str">"founded"</span>, <span class="str">"App"</span>, <span class="str">"##le"</span>, SEP]\n'
+ '<span class="fn">word_ids</span>(): [<span class="kw">None</span>,  <span class="num">0</span>,      <span class="num">1</span>,    <span class="num">1</span>,       <span class="num">2</span>,         <span class="num">3</span>,     <span class="num">3</span>,    <span class="kw">None</span>]\n'
+ '\n'
+ '<span class="cm"># Resulting aligned labels:</span>\n'
+ 'Labels:     [<span class="num">-100</span>, B-PER, I-PER, <span class="num">-100</span>,   O,         B-ORG, <span class="num">-100</span>, <span class="num">-100</span>]\n'
+ '<span class="cm">#            ^^^^                  ^^^^                      ^^^^  ^^^^</span>\n'
+ '<span class="cm">#            CLS   first  subword  subword  word       first  sub   SEP</span>\n'
+ '<span class="cm">#            →-100 sub    of w1    of w1    3→O        sub    of w3 →-100</span>\n'
+ '<span class="cm">#                  of w0  gets     ignored  gets       of w3  ignored</span>\n'
+ '<span class="cm">#                  gets   I label  (-100)   its label  gets   (-100)</span>\n'
+ '<span class="cm">#                  B-PER  (or -100)                    B-ORG</span>'
+ '</code></pre></div></div>'
+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>Reading the subword alignment trace:</strong> 1) The original word list has four labels (<code>B-PER</code>, <code>I-PER</code>, <code>O</code>, <code>B-ORG</code>). 2) After BERT tokenization, "Jobs" becomes <code>"Jo"</code> + <code>"##bs"</code> and "Apple" becomes <code>"App"</code> + <code>"##le"</code>; <code>word_ids()</code> reports <code>[None, 0, 1, 1, 2, 3, 3, None]</code> — <code>None</code> for special tokens and repeated indices for continuation subwords. 3) The aligned label sequence emits the real BIO tag only at the first subword of each word and <code>-100</code> everywhere else, so PyTorch\'s cross-entropy loss completely ignores special tokens and continuation pieces.</p>'
+ '</div>'

